1
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:06,320
This sandwich is an unmitigated disaster.

2
00:00:06,700 --> 00:00:08,410
I asked for turkey and roast beef

3
00:00:08,630 --> 00:00:10,350
With lettuce and swiss on whole wheat.

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:11,150
What did they give you?

5
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:12,380
Turkey and roast beef

6
00:00:12,600 --> 00:00:15,180
With swiss and lettuce on whole wheat.

7
00:00:19,730 --> 00:00:23,180
It's the right ingredients, but in the wrong order.

8
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,510
In a proper sandwich,

9
00:00:25,730 --> 00:00:27,410
The cheese is adjacent to the bread

10
00:00:27,630 --> 00:00:30,480
To create a moisture barrier against the lettuce.

11
00:00:30,700 --> 00:00:34,280
They might as well have dragged this thing through a car wash.

12
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:36,250
I don't believe it.

13
00:00:36,460 --> 00:00:38,150
I know. it's basic culinary science.

14
00:00:38,360 --> 00:00:41,280
Some guy is auctioning off a miniature time machine prop

15
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:43,880
From the original film, and no one is bidding on it.

16
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:46,810
A time machine from the movie the time machine?

17
00:00:47,030 --> 00:00:50,680
No. a time machine from sophie's choice.

18
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:53,210
Boy, sophie could have used a time machine in that movie.

19
00:00:53,430 --> 00:00:55,510
Did you see it? it's rough.

20
00:00:55,730 --> 00:00:57,610
Oh, that's cool.

21
00:00:57,830 --> 00:00:59,650
It's only $800?

22
00:00:59,860 --> 00:01:01,110
Yeah. and that's my bid.

23
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:02,450
You bid $800?

24
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:04,110
It was a spur-of-the-moment thing.

25
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:05,610
I figured it would go for thousands,

26
00:01:05,830 --> 00:01:07,510
And I just wanted to be a part of it.

27
00:01:07,730 --> 00:01:09,650
There's only 30 seconds left in the auction.

28
00:01:09,860 --> 00:01:10,810
Do you have $800?

29
00:01:11,030 --> 00:01:13,180
Not to blow on a miniature time machine.

30
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:14,450
Well, don't worry. the way these things work

31
00:01:14,660 --> 00:01:15,910
Is people wait until the last second to bid,

32
00:01:16,130 --> 00:01:17,750
And then they swoop in and get it. it's called sniping.

33
00:01:17,960 --> 00:01:18,980
15 seconds.

34
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:21,550
Come on, snipers.

35
00:01:24,460 --> 00:01:25,880
Where are your snipers?

36
00:01:26,100 --> 00:01:27,810
Five... snipe.

37
00:01:28,030 --> 00:01:28,810
Four... snipe.

38
00:01:29,030 --> 00:01:29,880
Three... snipe!

39
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:30,950
Two... snipe!

40
00:01:32,630 --> 00:01:34,380
Congratulations!

41
00:01:34,600 --> 00:01:37,050
You are the proud owner of a miniature time machine!

42
00:01:38,530 --> 00:01:40,080
You lucky duck.

43
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:42,250
I wonder why no one else bid.

44
00:01:42,460 --> 00:01:44,680
This is a classic piece of sci-fi movie memorabilia.

45
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:46,580
Yeah, I know! I still can't afford it.

46
00:01:46,800 --> 00:01:47,880
Why don't we share it?

47
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:49,310
We'll each put in 200 bucks, and we'll

48
00:01:49,530 --> 00:01:51,080
Take turns having it in our house.

49
00:01:51,300 --> 00:01:53,180
A time-share time machine.

50
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:55,580
I'm in. sheldon?

51
00:01:55,800 --> 00:01:57,680
Need you ask?

52
00:01:57,900 --> 00:02:00,150
I still don't understand why no one else bid.

53
00:02:09,260 --> 00:02:12,250
I understand why no one else bid.

54
00:02:30,630 --> 00:02:33,630
The.Nerdvana.Annihilation

55
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:42,790
Did the listing actually say "miniature"?

56
00:02:44,470 --> 00:02:46,750
I just assumed.

57
00:02:46,970 --> 00:02:51,790
Who sells a full-size time machine for $800?

58
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,350
In a venn diagram, that would be an individual located

59
00:02:56,570 --> 00:02:57,720
Within the intersection of the sets "no longer want my time machine"

60
00:02:57,940 --> 00:03:00,890
And "need $800."

61
00:03:01,100 --> 00:03:03,320
It's actually a tremendous bargain.

62
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:04,390
Even with shipping, it works out

63
00:03:04,600 --> 00:03:07,320
To less than four dollars a pound.

64
00:03:07,540 --> 00:03:10,950
Cocktail shrimp are $12.50.

65
00:03:11,170 --> 00:03:13,620
How are we going to get it upstairs?

66
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:16,550
If we take the dish off,

67
00:03:16,770 --> 00:03:18,050
It might fit in the elevator.

68
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:21,120
Yes, but the elevator has been broken for two years.

69
00:03:21,340 --> 00:03:22,450
I've been meaning to ask you.

70
00:03:22,670 --> 00:03:24,620
Do you think we should make a call about that?

71
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:27,590
Not necessary.

72
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:29,320
I have a master's in engineering.

73
00:03:29,540 --> 00:03:32,050
I remotely repair satellites on a regular basis.

74
00:03:32,270 --> 00:03:35,590
I troubleshoot space shuttle payloads.

75
00:03:35,800 --> 00:03:39,050
When the mars rover started pulling to the left, I performed

76
00:03:39,270 --> 00:03:43,490
A front-end alignment from 62 million miles away.

77
00:03:53,370 --> 00:03:55,220
No. that baby's broken.

78
00:03:57,020 --> 00:03:59,290
Come on, guys! push!

79
00:03:59,290 --> 00:04:00,480
If I push any harder,

80
00:04:00,690 --> 00:04:03,610
I'm going to give birth to my colon.

81
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:05,540
I can't feel my fingers. hurry up!

82
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:06,610
It's the same amount of work

83
00:04:06,830 --> 00:04:07,940
No matter how fast you go.

84
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:09,110
Basic physics.

85
00:04:09,330 --> 00:04:09,940
Sheldon? yeah?

86
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:11,940
If my fingers ever work again,

87
00:04:12,160 --> 00:04:14,440
I've got a job for the middle one.

88
00:04:14,660 --> 00:04:15,940
Oh. hey, guys.

89
00:04:16,160 --> 00:04:17,240
Oh. hi, penny.

90
00:04:20,030 --> 00:04:22,310
Take a break, guys.

91
00:04:22,530 --> 00:04:24,010
What are you doing?

92
00:04:24,230 --> 00:04:26,480
We're just, you know, moving something upstairs.

93
00:04:26,690 --> 00:04:27,480
What is it?

94
00:04:27,690 --> 00:04:31,140
Just... you know. time machine.

95
00:04:31,360 --> 00:04:33,740
Yeah. okay. neat. but I really got to get to work.

96
00:04:33,960 --> 00:04:35,610
Just give us a few minutes. I don't have a few minutes.

97
00:04:35,830 --> 00:04:36,810
I'm running really late.

98
00:04:37,030 --> 00:04:38,710
Then, I have a simple solution.

99
00:04:38,930 --> 00:04:40,980
Go up to the roof. hop over to the next building.

100
00:04:41,190 --> 00:04:42,410
There's a small gap--

101
00:04:42,630 --> 00:04:44,310
Don't look down if you're subject to vertigo--

102
00:04:44,530 --> 00:04:45,480
And use their stairwell.

103
00:04:45,690 --> 00:04:47,080
You're joking, right?

104
00:04:47,290 --> 00:04:49,140
Oh, I never joke when it comes to vertigo.

105
00:04:51,890 --> 00:04:54,780
Damn. okay, I'll just take the roof.

106
00:04:54,990 --> 00:04:56,680
Hey, if you wait for us to set up the time machine,

107
00:04:56,890 --> 00:04:58,740
I can drop you off at work yesterday.

108
00:05:00,730 --> 00:05:03,010
Time travel joke. it's not... never mind.

109
00:05:04,730 --> 00:05:07,440
For what it's worth, I thought it was humorous.

110
00:05:11,230 --> 00:05:12,840
Let's just do this.

111
00:05:13,060 --> 00:05:14,280
You guys ready to push?

112
00:05:14,490 --> 00:05:15,540
In a minute.

113
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:18,580
Howard stepped outside to throw up.

114
00:05:23,390 --> 00:05:25,080
I don't know what you were worried about.

115
00:05:25,290 --> 00:05:26,980
I think it really works in the room.

116
00:05:28,160 --> 00:05:29,210
Yeah.

117
00:05:29,430 --> 00:05:31,040
It is by far the coolest thing

118
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:32,380
I have ever owned.

119
00:05:32,590 --> 00:05:35,240
The exact time machine that carried actor rod taylor

120
00:05:35,460 --> 00:05:36,640
From victorian england

121
00:05:36,860 --> 00:05:38,580
Into the post-apocalyptic future,

122
00:05:38,790 --> 00:05:40,810
Where society had splintered into the two factions

123
00:05:41,030 --> 00:05:43,410
The subterranean morlocks, who survived by feasting

124
00:05:43,630 --> 00:05:46,410
On the flesh of the gentle surface-dwelling eloi.

125
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:50,610
Talk about your chick magnets.

126
00:05:52,890 --> 00:05:53,840
Oh, yeah.

127
00:05:54,060 --> 00:05:55,980
The guy who lives next to me is always like,

128
00:05:56,190 --> 00:05:57,580
"i have a jacuzzi on my balcony.

129
00:05:57,790 --> 00:05:59,540
I have a jacuzzi on my balcony."

130
00:05:59,760 --> 00:06:01,010
But wait until I tell him,

131
00:06:01,230 --> 00:06:03,440
"i've got a time machine on my balcony.

132
00:06:04,830 --> 00:06:07,180
Stuff that in your speedo, jacuzzi bob."

133
00:06:08,590 --> 00:06:10,480
Gentlemen, I know we said we'd take turns,

134
00:06:10,690 --> 00:06:11,610
But I think you'll agree

135
00:06:11,830 --> 00:06:14,010
That practicality dictates it remain here.

136
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:15,580
You can't just keep it here.

137
00:06:15,790 --> 00:06:16,840
What if I meet a girl and say,

138
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:18,410
"you want to come up and see my time machine?

139
00:06:18,630 --> 00:06:19,680
It's at my friends' house."

140
00:06:19,890 --> 00:06:21,610
How lame is that?

141
00:06:21,830 --> 00:06:23,340
He's got a point. all right,

142
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:25,780
I think we're going to need some ground rules; in addition

143
00:06:25,990 --> 00:06:27,380
To the expected "no shoes in the time machine,"

144
00:06:27,590 --> 00:06:29,280
And "no eating in the time machine,"

145
00:06:29,490 --> 00:06:31,140
I propose we add "pants must be worn

146
00:06:31,360 --> 00:06:33,740
At all times in the time machine."

147
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:35,940
Seconded.

148
00:06:36,160 --> 00:06:37,640
I was going to put down a towel.

149
00:06:41,830 --> 00:06:43,310
I still want it on my balcony.

150
00:06:43,530 --> 00:06:45,740
I say we move it on a bi-monthly basis.

151
00:06:45,960 --> 00:06:46,910
Yeah, that sounds fair.

152
00:06:47,130 --> 00:06:48,880
Hold on, bi-monthly is an ambiguous term.

153
00:06:49,090 --> 00:06:50,880
Do you mean move it every other month or twice a month?

154
00:06:51,090 --> 00:06:52,280
Twice a month. then no.

155
00:06:52,490 --> 00:06:53,780
Okay, every other month.

156
00:06:53,990 --> 00:06:54,980
No.

157
00:06:56,090 --> 00:06:57,740
Sheldon, you can't be selfish.

158
00:06:57,960 --> 00:07:00,240
We all paid for it, so it belongs to all of us.

159
00:07:00,460 --> 00:07:02,240
Now get out of the way so I can sit in my time machine.

160
00:07:12,130 --> 00:07:17,110
Okay, I am setting the dials for march 10, 1876.

161
00:07:17,330 --> 00:07:18,910
Good choice, alexander graham bell

162
00:07:19,130 --> 00:07:21,010
Invents the telephone and calls out for dr. watson.

163
00:07:21,230 --> 00:07:22,980
Wait a minute. I'd want to see that, too.

164
00:07:23,190 --> 00:07:24,510
So when it's your turn, you can.

165
00:07:24,730 --> 00:07:26,410
But if we all go back to the same point in time,

166
00:07:26,630 --> 00:07:28,380
Bell's lab is going to get very crowded.

167
00:07:28,590 --> 00:07:29,510
He'll know something's up.

168
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:32,640
Also since the time machine doesn't move in space,

169
00:07:32,860 --> 00:07:35,180
You'll end up in 1876 pasadena.

170
00:07:35,390 --> 00:07:37,110
And even if you can make it to boston, what are you going to do?

171
00:07:37,330 --> 00:07:38,280
Knock on the door and say to mrs. bell,

172
00:07:38,490 --> 00:07:39,910
"hey, mrs. bell. big fan of your husband.

173
00:07:40,130 --> 00:07:42,680
Can I come in and watch him invent the telephone?"

174
00:07:42,890 --> 00:07:43,840
Mrs. bell was deaf.

175
00:07:44,060 --> 00:07:45,180
She's not even going to hear you knock.

176
00:07:46,660 --> 00:07:48,010
I have a solution.

177
00:07:48,230 --> 00:07:51,280
First, go into the future and obtain a cloaking device.

178
00:07:51,490 --> 00:07:52,310
Ooh, how far into the future?

179
00:07:52,530 --> 00:07:53,480
If I remember correctly,

180
00:07:53,690 --> 00:07:55,340
Captain kirk will steal a cloaking device

181
00:07:55,560 --> 00:07:58,340
From the romulans on stardate 5027.3,

182
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:01,810
Which would be january 10, 2328 by pre-federation reckoning.

183
00:08:03,590 --> 00:08:08,410
Okay, I am setting the dials for january 10, 2328.

184
00:08:08,630 --> 00:08:11,910
Here we go into the future.

185
00:08:32,530 --> 00:08:33,410
That was fun.

186
00:08:35,030 --> 00:08:35,940
My turn!

187
00:08:37,130 --> 00:08:39,110
Okay, first of all,

188
00:08:39,330 --> 00:08:41,610
What you call a gap was nearly three feet wide.

189
00:08:41,830 --> 00:08:43,440
I slipped and skinned my knee. are you okay?

190
00:08:45,030 --> 00:08:46,480
Second of all, the door to the stairwell

191
00:08:46,690 --> 00:08:47,740
Of the other building was locked,

192
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:49,210
So I had to go down the fire escape,

193
00:08:49,430 --> 00:08:50,340
Which ends on the third floor,

194
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:51,980
Forcing me to crawl through the window

195
00:08:52,190 --> 00:08:53,640
Of a lovely armenian family

196
00:08:53,860 --> 00:08:56,010
Who insisted I stay for lunch.

197
00:08:56,230 --> 00:08:57,910
That doesn't sound too bad.

198
00:08:58,130 --> 00:08:59,610
It was eight courses of lamb,

199
00:08:59,830 --> 00:09:02,340
And they tried to fix me up with their son.

200
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:05,080
I'm sorry. not done.

201
00:09:05,290 --> 00:09:06,510
By the time I finally

202
00:09:06,730 --> 00:09:07,910
Got to work, they'd given my shift away.

203
00:09:08,130 --> 00:09:09,110
Yeah, that's right.

204
00:09:09,330 --> 00:09:11,410
I lost an entire day's pay thanks to this.

205
00:09:11,630 --> 00:09:13,280
This... time machine.

206
00:09:16,790 --> 00:09:18,710
The lights flash and the dish spins. you want to try it?

207
00:09:18,930 --> 00:09:20,080
No!

208
00:09:20,290 --> 00:09:21,980
I don't want to try it!

209
00:09:22,190 --> 00:09:24,640
My god, you are grown men!

210
00:09:24,860 --> 00:09:26,380
How can you waste your lives with these

211
00:09:26,590 --> 00:09:28,210
Stupid toys and costumes

212
00:09:28,430 --> 00:09:30,210
And comic books and... and now that?!

213
00:09:30,430 --> 00:09:33,010
That... again. time machine.

214
00:09:35,260 --> 00:09:37,010
Oh, please, it's not a time machine.

215
00:09:37,230 --> 00:09:38,210
If anything, it looks like something

216
00:09:38,430 --> 00:09:40,640
Elton john would drive through the everglades.

217
00:09:45,990 --> 00:09:48,440
It only moves in time.

218
00:09:48,660 --> 00:09:51,840
It would be worse than useless in a swamp.

219
00:09:56,530 --> 00:09:58,440
Pathetic! all of you!

220
00:09:58,660 --> 00:10:00,510
Completely pathetic!

221
00:10:05,690 --> 00:10:06,880
My turn.

222
00:10:24,930 --> 00:10:26,840
Leonard, it's 2:00 in the morning.

223
00:10:27,060 --> 00:10:29,380
So?

224
00:10:29,590 --> 00:10:30,740
So it's my turn.

225
00:10:33,630 --> 00:10:35,880
Why did you set it for the day before yesterday?

226
00:10:36,090 --> 00:10:38,880
Because I want to go back and keep myself

227
00:10:39,090 --> 00:10:40,410
From getting a time machine.

228
00:10:40,630 --> 00:10:42,210
You can't. if you were to prevent yourself

229
00:10:42,430 --> 00:10:43,440
From buying it in the past,

230
00:10:43,660 --> 00:10:45,310
You would not have it available in the present

231
00:10:45,530 --> 00:10:46,540
To travel back and stop yourself

232
00:10:46,760 --> 00:10:48,710
From buying it. ergo, you would still have it.

233
00:10:48,930 --> 00:10:50,940
This is a classic rookie time-travel mistake.

234
00:10:54,660 --> 00:10:57,680
Can I go back and prevent you from explaining that to me?

235
00:10:57,890 --> 00:10:59,210
Same paradox.

236
00:10:59,430 --> 00:11:00,840
If you were to travel back in time

237
00:11:01,060 --> 00:11:02,240
And, say, knock me unconscious,

238
00:11:02,460 --> 00:11:03,710
You would not then have the conversation

239
00:11:03,930 --> 00:11:05,180
That irritated you, motivating you

240
00:11:05,390 --> 00:11:07,510
To go back and knock me unconscious.

241
00:11:07,730 --> 00:11:11,240
What if I knocked you unconscious right now?

242
00:11:12,730 --> 00:11:13,910
It won't change the past.

243
00:11:16,490 --> 00:11:18,710
But it'd make the present so much nicer.

244
00:11:19,890 --> 00:11:22,210
Are you upset about something?

245
00:11:22,430 --> 00:11:24,240
What was your first clue?

246
00:11:24,460 --> 00:11:26,880
Well, it was a number of things.

247
00:11:27,090 --> 00:11:28,210
First, the late hour.

248
00:11:28,430 --> 00:11:29,540
Then your demeanor seems very low-energy,

249
00:11:29,550 --> 00:11:30,340
Plus your irritability.

250
00:11:30,550 --> 00:11:31,340
Yes, I'm upset!

251
00:11:32,890 --> 00:11:35,340
I don't usually pick up on those things.

252
00:11:35,550 --> 00:11:36,570
Good for me.

253
00:11:39,590 --> 00:11:40,940
Yeah, good for you.

254
00:11:42,120 --> 00:11:43,200
Oh, wait.

255
00:11:45,220 --> 00:11:47,400
Did you want to talk about what's bothering you?

256
00:11:48,890 --> 00:11:49,670
I don't know. maybe.

257
00:11:49,890 --> 00:11:51,470
Wow. I'm on fire tonight.

258
00:11:56,290 --> 00:11:58,270
Uh... here's the thing.

259
00:11:58,490 --> 00:12:02,770
Girls like penny never end up with guys who own time machines.

260
00:12:02,990 --> 00:12:04,640
I disagree.

261
00:12:04,850 --> 00:12:06,970
Your inability to successfully woo penny

262
00:12:07,190 --> 00:12:10,270
Long predates your acquisition of the time machine.

263
00:12:12,350 --> 00:12:14,940
That failure clearly stands on its own.

264
00:12:15,150 --> 00:12:17,140
Thanks for pointing it out.

265
00:12:17,350 --> 00:12:19,170
In addition, your premise is flawed.

266
00:12:19,390 --> 00:12:22,100
In the original film, rod taylor got yvette mimieux

267
00:12:22,320 --> 00:12:23,700
With that very time machine.

268
